//Deep Copy

Deep copy means creating a completely separate copy of the data.

Unlike shallow copy, the pointer is not copied directly. New memory is allocated and the value is copied into it.

Example:

class Student {
public:
    int* age;

    Student(int a) {
        age = new int(a);
    }

    Student(const Student& s) {
        age = new int(*s.age);
    }
};

Now:

Student s1(20);
Student s2 = s1;

Memory looks like:

s1.age ──→ [20]

s2.age ──→ [20]

They have different memory locations.

So:

*s2.age = 25;

will not change s1.age.

Shallow vs Deep
Shallow Copy:
s1 ──→ [20] ←── s2
       same memory

Deep Copy:
s1 ──→ [20]

s2 ──→ [20]
       separate memory
Simple definition

Deep copy = copying the actual data into newly allocated, independent memory.

Deep copy is mainly needed when a class contains raw pointers/dynamically allocated resources.